The main change is in the path for message numbers of 1024 and above. Before, every lookup there scanned the whole `mesgs` array. Now, while the static index is built once, it also records whether any profile message has a number that large. If none does, which is likely the case for the standard profile, unknown high numbers return `NULL` immediately with no scan.

Behavior is unchanged:
- For numbers below 1024 the table still stores the first matching index, so first-match semantics hold.
- For numbers of 1024 and above, the linear scan still runs whenever the profile does contain such entries.

The hot path for numbers below 1024 stays a single 2 KB table lookup, with a branch hint marking the miss case as unlikely. I have not measured the speed-up.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    enum { SMALL = 1024 };
    struct Index
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[SMALL];
        bool hasLarge;
        Index()
        {
            hasLarge = false;
            for (int k = 0; k < SMALL; k++)
                idx[k] = 0xFFFF;
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
            {
                if (mesgs[i].num < SMALL)
                    idx[mesgs[i].num] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
                else
                    hasLarge = true;
            }
        }
    };
    static const Index index;

    if (__builtin_expect(num < SMALL, 1))
    {
        const FIT_UINT16 i = index.idx[num];
        if (__builtin_expect(i == 0xFFFF, 0))
            return NULL;
        return &mesgs[i];
    }

    if (!index.hasLarge)
        return NULL;

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }
    return NULL;
}
```